charley hardman[060109]saltypig@saltypig.com[060109]http://saltypig.com/blog/[060109]i naturally root for walt anderson, and am thankful thomas olson wrote the article. but notice the ingrained language so easy to fall into, even from liberty advocates: "his taxes". partially correct, in that they are taxes on him, but to assign them as his, and even to refer to them as taxes, loses the most important battle -- language. i owe no taxes. i have no taxes i am to pay. it is <a href="http://www.m-w.com/cgi-bin/dictionary?book=Dictionary&va=extort">extortion</a>. the IRS is a racket. ("a : a fraudulent scheme, enterprise, or activity b : a usually illegitimate enterprise made workable by bribery or intimidation c : an easy and lucrative means of livelihood") -- <a href="http://www.strike-the-root.com/4/stevens/stevens3.html">a hoax</a> (link highly recommended).<BR><BR>however, that doesn't keep "good people" at the IRS from pretending otherwise and swinging the gun barrel to my head. nothing exposes the brutality of these smirking beasts so much as their assembling of dominos for the last crush of a man. they are sadistic and unworthy of life -- picking off those unfortunate enough to have been born disposed to consider shared liberty the essential companion of air. how many will refuse to be robbed? it's a tough damn road. "suicidal" is one way of looking at it.<BR><BR>however, another way of looking is that it's the opposite of suicide.<BR><BR>"The light that burns twice as bright burns half as long. And you have burned so very very brightly, Roy."<BR><BR>there's a lot to be learned from the story of walt anderson. thanks for blogging it. hope it gets through.<BR><BR>===<BR>BTW, bill, did you happen to catch the <a href="http://www.crooksandliars.com/2006/01/04.html#a6571"bill o'reilly interview on letterman</a>? something remotely refreshing (still not hopeful, i'm afraid) is happening in this country, much as i still don't agree with letterman (nor cindy sheehan). forget the BOR nonsense; it was shocking to see letterman defying l'emporer bush to an initial audience of ~4 million.<BR><BR>forget bush though; crush the IRS, and the bushes will follow.[060109]01-09-2006 14:28
